If I may ask: why?  If your goal is to achieve that first
ascension: Wizard is doable, but why Orc?

> Anyway, if I could get some advice specific to Orc Wizards, I
> would appreciate it.  I pick Orcs partly for the oddness, but
> I also think the starting poison resistance makes-up for the
> max 16 INT - my Orc Wizards rarely starve.

It's been ages since I've had a character starve.  It's true,
that Wizards are prime candidates, since they spellcast from the
start.  Still, as a Wizard, I tend to avoid fighting at all, and
let my pet handle the monsters.  In the early game, good armor
is critical, and the good armor you get in the early game
doesn't allow spellcasting.  The Wizards that I've ascended have
all started by hiding behind their pet until they've gotten a
few daggers, then throwing them, and only spellcasting later.

> Here's my basic strategy at this point:

> * Try to get a dagger for a primary weapon ASAP to start
> building that weapon skill.  Often this means killing a
> friendly goblin - otherwise I can usually get some in the
> Gnomish Mines.

Your pet should kill the monster for you.  (Not that I've
anything against killing a friendly if he has something I want.
Especially if I'm chaotic.)

> * Do the first couple of levels of the Gnomish Mines for the
> daggers, tools, and experience, but don't try to complete it
> yet.  If I am doing well, I will try to make it to Minetown
> for the altar, in case it happens to be co-aligned.

General rule: don't go into the mines until the pet is no longer
"little".

    [...]
> I've only had enough money at the Minetown altar to buy
> protection once so far.

You'll usually be able to get a pick-axe in the mines.  After
that, a little bit of digging where there are vaults, plus the
final level of Sobokan, should result in enough loot for one
level of protection.

> Any tips on survivability, making money, or getting AC down
> other than protection or wishing for dragon scale mail
> (without impeding spellcasting)?

Given the choice, in the early game, your best bet is still
mithral and daggers, and leave the spell casting until later.
(In general, anyway.  If you do get an early wish, or stumble on
some highly enchanted armor which allows a decent AC without
inhibiting spellcasting, then the decisions are different.)

